  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Wednesday/... \nDuring the first half of next week, the anti-cyclone center will \npropagate across the four corners into central Colorado allowing \nenhanced easterly flow and better chances of inverted shortwaves \nentering the SW Conus. However, there is considerable uncertainty \nregarding the timing and location of any particular feature which \nwould enhance thunderstorm chances, not to mention mesoscale details \nfrom decayed convective complexes which further complicates the \nforecast. However, as early as Saturday night, long tracking, strong \noutflows may be sufficient to incur isolated storm development over \nlower elevations, while deepening moisture profiles, increasing \ntotal column PWATs, and setting the stage for enhanced convective \ncoverage on following days. Various modeling suites highlight \ndifferent days with a more preferred chance of storms sweeping into \nlower elevations early next week, but all models indicate at least \none easterly wave influencing enhanced activity. \n\nThis easterly flow will also help erode higher midlevel heights and \ntemperatures; and combined with the convective influences, may \nsignificantly reduce and complicate heat related impacts. A growing \ntrend in models shows outflows and convection rapidly cooling \ntemperatures across Arizona early in the week such that by Monday, \nreadings return to normal levels. GFS BUFR soundings are even more \naggressive towards outflows affecting temperatures suggesting \nextensive cloud cover much of the day Sunday, and readings falling \nwell short of 110F. Not putting too much stock in these types of \ndetails 3-4 days in advance, but certainly the pattern supports this \nevolution where somewhat lower temperatures and a return to only \nmoderate HeatRisk arrives quicker than previously forecast.",
